[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [{:db/id 17592186172135 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-01a5e642-9803-5a1b-8846-85c812cf1513"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-01a5e642-9803-5a1b-8846-85c812cf1513" :question/id #uuid "01a5e642-9803-5a1b-8846-85c812cf1513" :question/math-academy-id "q-162815" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find the minimum of the function $f(x,y,z)=xy-zy$ on the intersection of the surfaces $y+z=1$ and ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨y,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,-y⟩=\\lambda ⟨0,\\,1,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩ \\\\ y+z=1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the expressions?" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy-zy\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=y+z=1,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨y,\\,x-z,\\,-y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d2718901-61ad-591c-ba8b-c2899ee3b35f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d2718901-61ad-591c-ba8b-c2899ee3b35f" :answer-field/id #uuid "d2718901-61ad-591c-ba8b-c2899ee3b35f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4899d2f0-4a9a-54c0-b255-243f00296fb1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7c3d9025-0e2b-5e52-b2d1-c27a27ac63c7" "answer-900204f4-4ee6-51fa-bded-94c975db4cc9" "answer-4899d2f0-4a9a-54c0-b255-243f00296fb1" "answer-d5255563-50f7-52ae-bccd-46caade6840e" "answer-7307529a-c5fa-50c3-85e4-0c320e676b56"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7c3d9025-0e2b-5e52-b2d1-c27a27ac63c7" :answer/id #uuid "7c3d9025-0e2b-5e52-b2d1-c27a27ac63c7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-2z,\\,$ $0,\\,$  and $2z$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-900204f4-4ee6-51fa-bded-94c975db4cc9" :answer/id #uuid "900204f4-4ee6-51fa-bded-94c975db4cc9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2z,\\,$ $1,\\,$  and $2z$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4899d2f0-4a9a-54c0-b255-243f00296fb1" :answer/id #uuid "4899d2f0-4a9a-54c0-b255-243f00296fb1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x-z,\\,$ $1,\\,$  and $2z$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d5255563-50f7-52ae-bccd-46caade6840e" :answer/id #uuid "d5255563-50f7-52ae-bccd-46caade6840e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$xz,\\,$ $0,\\,$  and $2z$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7307529a-c5fa-50c3-85e4-0c320e676b56" :answer/id #uuid "7307529a-c5fa-50c3-85e4-0c320e676b56" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$yz,\\,$ $1,\\,$  and $z$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173258 :tutorial/content "We can find global extrema of multivariable functions subject to multiple constraints using the method of Lagrange multipliers. \n\nFor example, suppose that we want to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = 4xy+z^2\n$$\n\nsubject to the following *two* constraints: \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x^2+y^2=1, \\qquad h(x,y,z)=2x^2 + 4y^2 - z = 0\n$$\n\nGeometrically, this means that we want to minimize $f(x,y,z)$ over the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=1$ and the elliptic paraboloid $2x^2 + 4y^2 - z = 0,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/0b/0b16f73e034724a9f38d91a606f627db85299507552c45192e91c39bc0c6725a.png)\n\nThe method of Lagrange multipliers states that the extrema of the function $f(x,y,z)$ subject to constraints $g(x,y,z)=a$ and $h(x,y,z)=b$ can be found by solving the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=a \\\\ h(x,y,z)=b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the constants $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$ are the Lagrange multipliers of this system.\n\nAs before, this method assumes that $\\nabla g \\neq \\mathbf{0}$ and $\\nabla h \\neq \\mathbf{0}$ on the curves $g(x,y,z)=a$ and $h(x,y,z)=b,$ and that the extreme values exist.\n\n**Note:** Geometrically, the condition $\\nabla f = \\lambda \\nabla g + \\mu \\nabla h$ states that\n\n- $\\nabla f$ lies in the plane spanned by $\\nabla g$ and $\\nabla h,$ or, equivalently,\n\n- $\\nabla f$ is perpendicular to the level surfaces $g(x,y,z) = a$ and $h(x,y,z) = b.$\n\nWe'll explore this geometric intuition further at the end of the lesson." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]}] "assertions" [["question-01a5e642-9803-5a1b-8846-85c812cf1513" :question/id #uuid "01a5e642-9803-5a1b-8846-85c812cf1513"] ["question-01a5e642-9803-5a1b-8846-85c812cf1513" :question/math-academy-id "q-162815"] ["question-01a5e642-9803-5a1b-8846-85c812cf1513" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find the minimum of the function $f(x,y,z)=xy-zy$ on the intersection of the surfaces $y+z=1$ and ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨y,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,-y⟩=\\lambda ⟨0,\\,1,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩ \\\\ y+z=1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the expressions?"] ["question-01a5e642-9803-5a1b-8846-85c812cf1513" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy-zy\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=y+z=1,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨y,\\,x-z,\\,-y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] ["question-01a5e642-9803-5a1b-8846-85c812cf1513" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-d2718901-61ad-591c-ba8b-c2899ee3b35f" :answer-field/id #uuid "d2718901-61ad-591c-ba8b-c2899ee3b35f"] ["answer-7c3d9025-0e2b-5e52-b2d1-c27a27ac63c7" :answer/id #uuid "7c3d9025-0e2b-5e52-b2d1-c27a27ac63c7"] ["answer-7c3d9025-0e2b-5e52-b2d1-c27a27ac63c7" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-7c3d9025-0e2b-5e52-b2d1-c27a27ac63c7" :answer/value "$-2z,\\,$ $0,\\,$  and $2z$"] ["answer-900204f4-4ee6-51fa-bded-94c975db4cc9" :answer/id #uuid "900204f4-4ee6-51fa-bded-94c975db4cc9"] ["answer-900204f4-4ee6-51fa-bded-94c975db4cc9" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-900204f4-4ee6-51fa-bded-94c975db4cc9" :answer/value "$2z,\\,$ $1,\\,$  and $2z$"] ["answer-4899d2f0-4a9a-54c0-b255-243f00296fb1" :answer/id #uuid "4899d2f0-4a9a-54c0-b255-243f00296fb1"] ["answer-4899d2f0-4a9a-54c0-b255-243f00296fb1" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-4899d2f0-4a9a-54c0-b255-243f00296fb1" :answer/value "$x-z,\\,$ $1,\\,$  and $2z$"] ["answer-d5255563-50f7-52ae-bccd-46caade6840e" :answer/id #uuid "d5255563-50f7-52ae-bccd-46caade6840e"] ["answer-d5255563-50f7-52ae-bccd-46caade6840e" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-d5255563-50f7-52ae-bccd-46caade6840e" :answer/value "$xz,\\,$ $0,\\,$  and $2z$"] ["answer-7307529a-c5fa-50c3-85e4-0c320e676b56" :answer/id #uuid "7307529a-c5fa-50c3-85e4-0c320e676b56"] ["answer-7307529a-c5fa-50c3-85e4-0c320e676b56" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-7307529a-c5fa-50c3-85e4-0c320e676b56" :answer/value "$yz,\\,$ $1,\\,$  and $z$"] ["answer-field-d2718901-61ad-591c-ba8b-c2899ee3b35f" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-d2718901-61ad-591c-ba8b-c2899ee3b35f" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-d2718901-61ad-591c-ba8b-c2899ee3b35f" :answer-field/correct "answer-4899d2f0-4a9a-54c0-b255-243f00296fb1"] ["answer-field-d2718901-61ad-591c-ba8b-c2899ee3b35f" :answer-field/choices "answer-7c3d9025-0e2b-5e52-b2d1-c27a27ac63c7"] ["answer-field-d2718901-61ad-591c-ba8b-c2899ee3b35f" :answer-field/choices "answer-900204f4-4ee6-51fa-bded-94c975db4cc9"] ["answer-field-d2718901-61ad-591c-ba8b-c2899ee3b35f" :answer-field/choices "answer-4899d2f0-4a9a-54c0-b255-243f00296fb1"] ["answer-field-d2718901-61ad-591c-ba8b-c2899ee3b35f" :answer-field/choices "answer-d5255563-50f7-52ae-bccd-46caade6840e"] ["answer-field-d2718901-61ad-591c-ba8b-c2899ee3b35f" :answer-field/choices "answer-7307529a-c5fa-50c3-85e4-0c320e676b56"] ["question-01a5e642-9803-5a1b-8846-85c812cf1513" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-d2718901-61ad-591c-ba8b-c2899ee3b35f"] [17592186172135 :knowledge-point/questions "question-01a5e642-9803-5a1b-8846-85c812cf1513"] [17592186173258 :tutorial/content "We can find global extrema of multivariable functions subject to multiple constraints using the method of Lagrange multipliers. \n\nFor example, suppose that we want to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = 4xy+z^2\n$$\n\nsubject to the following *two* constraints: \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x^2+y^2=1, \\qquad h(x,y,z)=2x^2 + 4y^2 - z = 0\n$$\n\nGeometrically, this means that we want to minimize $f(x,y,z)$ over the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=1$ and the elliptic paraboloid $2x^2 + 4y^2 - z = 0,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/0b/0b16f73e034724a9f38d91a606f627db85299507552c45192e91c39bc0c6725a.png)\n\nThe method of Lagrange multipliers states that the extrema of the function $f(x,y,z)$ subject to constraints $g(x,y,z)=a$ and $h(x,y,z)=b$ can be found by solving the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=a \\\\ h(x,y,z)=b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the constants $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$ are the Lagrange multipliers of this system.\n\nAs before, this method assumes that $\\nabla g \\neq \\mathbf{0}$ and $\\nabla h \\neq \\mathbf{0}$ on the curves $g(x,y,z)=a$ and $h(x,y,z)=b,$ and that the extreme values exist.\n\n**Note:** Geometrically, the condition $\\nabla f = \\lambda \\nabla g + \\mu \\nabla h$ states that\n\n- $\\nabla f$ lies in the plane spanned by $\\nabla g$ and $\\nabla h,$ or, equivalently,\n\n- $\\nabla f$ is perpendicular to the level surfaces $g(x,y,z) = a$ and $h(x,y,z) = b.$\n\nWe'll explore this geometric intuition further at the end of the lesson."]] "retractions" [[17592186173258 :tutorial/content "We can find global extrema of multivariable functions subject to multiple constraints using the method of Lagrange multipliers. \n\nFor example, suppose that we want to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = 4xy+z^2\n$$\n\nsubject to the following *two* constraints: \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x^2+y^2=1, \\qquad h(x,y,z)=2x^2 + 4y^2 - z = 0\n$$\n\nGeometrically, this means that we want to minimize $f(x,y,z)$ over the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=1$ and the elliptic paraboloid $2x^2 + 4y^2 - z = 0,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/0b/0b16f73e034724a9f38d91a606f627db85299507552c45192e91c39bc0c6725a.png)\n\nThe method of Lagrange multipliers states that the extrema of the function $f(x,y,z)$ subject to constraints $g(x,y,z)=a$ and $h(x,y,z)=b$ can be found by solving the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) &=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z) &=a \\\\ h(x,y,z) &=b\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, the constants $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$ are the Lagrange multipliers of this system.\n\nAs before, this method assumes that $\\nabla g \\neq \\mathbf{0}$ and $\\nabla h \\neq \\mathbf{0}$ on the curves $g(x,y,z)=a$ and $h(x,y,z)=b,$ and that the extreme values exist.\n\n**Note:** Geometrically, the condition $\\nabla f = \\lambda \\nabla g + \\mu \\nabla h$ states that\n\n- $\\nabla f$ lies in the plane spanned by $\\nabla g$ and $\\nabla h,$ or, equivalently,\n\n- $\\nabla f$ is perpendicular to the level surfaces $g(x,y,z) = a$ and $h(x,y,z) = b.$\n\nWe'll explore this geometric intuition further at the end of the lesson."]]}]